{"entries":[{"caption":"4  A proposal for Human 2.0: mapping the breast","key":"sec-mapping","order":{"number":1,"section":[4,0,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Selecting regions after the scan. Left: the sample split into thirds by the 3 × 3 water/fat (fattiest, middle, most water-rich). Middle: the mean curve of each third. Right: the distribution of the 3 × 3 water/fat over every point of the sample, the kind of whole-breast summary that could be compared between a patient’s two breasts.","key":"fig-map-regions","order":{"number":3,"section":[4,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"The Ulster map, one value per point. Left to right: the total scattered intensity (the sample against the empty holder), the fat band (q 13-15), the water band (q 19.5-22.5), and the water band over the fat band, with each point averaged with its neighbours (3 × 3). Grey points are off the sample; white points were not measured.","key":"fig-map-bands","order":{"number":1,"section":[4,2,0,0,0,0,0]}},{"caption":"Adding points improves the 1D curve. One point in the middle of the sample on its own, and the mean of its 3 × 3 and 5 × 5 neighbourhoods and of every point on the sample. The grey bands mark the fat and water bands.","key":"fig-map-summing","order":{"number":2,"section":[4,2,0,0,0,0,0]}}],"headings":["a-human-1.0-stopgap-the-water-peak-filter-during-scanning","the-recommendation-mapping","how-long-would-a-whole-breast-map-take","sec-mapping"],"options":{"chapter-id":"sec-mapping","chapters":true}}